   "cite": "sin-cos",
   "prompt": "<p>For each pair of integrals, determine which one is more difficult to evaluate. Explain your reasoning.</p><p>$\\int {\\sin }^{456}x\\;\\cos x\\;dx$ or $\\int {\\sin }^{2}x\\;{\\cos }^{2}x\\;dx$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "The second integral, $\\int {\\sin }^{2}x\\;{\\cos }^{2}x\\;dx,$ is more difficult. The first has an odd power of $\\cos x,$ so it is a <em>u</em>-substitution type: with $u=\\sin x$ and $du=\\cos x\\;dx$ it becomes $\\int u^{456}du=\\frac{1}{457}{\\sin }^{457}x+C.$ The second has only even powers, so strategy 3 applies: the power-reducing identities give ${\\sin }^{2}x\\;{\\cos }^{2}x=\\frac{1}{4}{\\sin }^{2}(2x)=\\frac{1}{8}(1-\\cos (4x)),$ and only then can it be integrated.",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
